## Slot vocabulary ({{slot-count}} slots inherited or declared)

Library memorials inherit `:mm/Memory` plus the **Library Card** typed-edge axes:

| Axis | Predicate(s) | What it captures |
|---|---|---|
| Documentation | `documents` / `documented-by` | Reference→library + inverse |
| Mereology | `has-part` / `part-of` | Umbrella↔topic, library↔subpages |
| Formal realization | `implements` / `implemented-by` | Library realizes a spec / paper / RFC |
| Lineage | `descends-from` / `ancestor-of` | Genetic relationship in design history |
| Dependencies | `consumes` / `consumed-by` | Library uses another library |
| Provenance | `created-by` | Author / maker |
| Research substrate | `cites` | Papers / talks / docs grounding the design |
| Methodology attribution | `provides-method-for` / `uses-method-in` | Method-graph |
| Domain tagging | `domain-tags` (free-form string) | Project-scoped retrieval |
| Design-space positioning | `disagrees-with` / `is-disagreed-with-by` | Controversial stances |
| Empirical grounding | `evidences` / `evidenced-by` | Performance / correctness evidence |

The audit invariants `library-card-empty` (warning) flags library memorials with body > 100 lines AND ≤2 typed-edge axes declared (excluding `documents` / `documented-by` / `related`); `library-without-resources` (info) flags libraries with no `cites:` to any `type: resource`.

{{slot-table}}

## Canonical structure — facade + subpages

Most library studies exceed 200 lines if written as a single file.  The established pattern is **facade + subpages**:

```
libraries/
├── <lib>.md                    (facade: pitch, architecture,
│                                when-to-reach, cross-library
│                                relationships, deep-dive links)
└── <lib>/
    ├── kernel.md                  (one subpage per concern)
    ├── architecture_evidence.md   (16-point audit)
    ├── ropes.md / api.md / …      (subsystem deep-reads)
    └── testing.md / patterns.md / …
```

Small libraries can stay single-file.  Larger libraries use the facade + subpages shape; mereology is captured via `has-part` / `part-of` typed edges between facade and subpages.

## Canonical body structure (facade)

```markdown
## Pitch

<One sentence + one paragraph.  The elevator thesis.>

## What makes it distinctive

<The specific architectural move that differentiates this library
from its ecosystem.>

## Niche vs. adjacent tools

<Competitive comparison grounded in the README's own claims.>

## Top-level layout

<`src/` tree with LOC counts.>

## Public API

<Primary namespace + canonical functions.>

## When to reach for it

<2–3 concrete scenarios.>

## Cross-library relationships

<What it depends on, what depends on it.>

## Deep-dive memorials

<Links to subpages.>

## See also

<Other memorials.>
```

## Aesthetic audit (when applicable)

Library studies that share an authoring lineage with an established aesthetic preference should include a verdict against that preference's claimed conventions.  Each verdict: ✅ confirmed / ⚠️ with nuance / ❌ not present / ⚪ not applicable, with file:line citation.  These audits feed back into the aesthetic preference memorial.

## Pitfalls

- **README paraphrase, not source study** — the point is to go to the code; if the study reads like a rewrite of the README, delete and redo
- **Missing cross-library-graph context** — what consumes this library?  What does it consume?  Load-bearing
- **No canonical examples for extracted patterns** — if the study finds 3 new patterns but doesn't cite file:line for each, the extraction is soft
- **Skipping the aesthetic audit** — each library study should contribute at least one nuance to the aesthetic preference memorial

## Composition with adjacent classes

- **`reference`** — references point at facilities; libraries study them deeply.  References may grow into libraries
- **`resource`** — library studies cite resources (primary sources, papers, READMEs)
- **`pattern`** — patterns ground themselves in library studies via canonical examples
- **`preference`** — library studies feed back into aesthetic preferences via the audit
- **`bug`** — library studies describe what works well; bug memorials describe what still needs work in the same library

